  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first run finished with status `ok`.\n\n**1. What the data holds** (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`)\n- About 14,000 web requests, dated 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03. The sample data is generated around the install date, so much of it is still in the future.\n- Response codes: 200 (12,832), 404 (801) and 503 (441). The only server error code in the data is 503.\n- `bytes` ranges from 0 to about 20 KB, averaging about 5.7 KB.\n- Each request also records the client IP, URL, file extension, source/destination country (`geo.src`/`geo.dest`), operating system (`machine.os`), user agent, referrer and tags.\n\n**2. Dashboard: \"EFF-c959c897 Web logs overview\"** (id `7755cd1d-1834-48e2-9dbd-dabef61b97f6`, 7 panels)\n- Four headline numbers: total requests, average bytes per request, unique client IPs, and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, as stacked bars broken down by response code.\n- Top 10 requested URLs.\n- Top 10 destination countries.\n\nThe default time range is the last 7 days through 60 days ahead, so the future-dated data shows up. The API accepted all seven panels, but I haven't opened the dashboard to check that the three charts render.\n\n**3. Rule: \"EFF-c959c897 5xx spike\"** (id `cc402461-1382-44c5-a5fa-cf3566b6fed9`)\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" in the last 5 minutes and fires when the count is above 10.\n- It counts every matching request in the window each time, including ones an earlier check already saw.\n- It has no actions, so when it fires the alert only appears in Kibana; nothing is sent anywhere. Add an email or Slack connector if you want notifications.\n\nThe request bodies I used are saved in the scratchpad as `dash.py` and `rule.json`.",
